Subject:  Misrepresenting Objectivism

Dave misprepresents Objectivism in his reply to Elias Israel below:

>From: dkrieger@Synopsys.COM (Dave Krieger)
>Subject: The Evidence of the Senses
>
>I think you are porting the argument to a different domain than was
>originally being discussed.  The assertion "The only reality we can know is
>the one we perceive" (model agnosticism) is considerably different from
>"Reality is what we perceive it as" (Objectivism).

Objectivism does not hold that reality is what we perceive it as, no matter
what Randroids may have told you.  It holds that reality is what we
perceive.  Period.  "As" implies what Kelley calls a "perceptual judgement,"
an identification of that which is perceived in conceptual form.

This entire misunderstanding is based upon failure to take this distinction
into account.  The instances of mistake, error, or cognitive failure that
people bring up to challenge the evidence of the sense invariably is an
instance where it is not the perception of reality that is at fault, but
the conceptual identification of it.

This is analogous to libertarians denying that, e.g., homelessness is a
market failure being met with the criticism that they're denying the existence
of homelessness.  I accept the truth of both homelessness and cognitive
failures, but homelessness is not a market failure, but a State failure, and,
e.g., the failure to identify an assassin camouflaged in bushes you walk past
who then jumps and kills you is not a perceptual failure, but a failure of
perceptul judgement.  You perceived the assassin; you just didn't judge his
identity correctly.

