He does say it will be impossible "probably forever" but if confronted
with Perry's cubic meter of nanocomputers (code name: Inferno) might
well give ground.  He's just trying to say that reality-level
simulation by computer is Really Hard because of all the interactions
that must be modeled (he refers to it as a combinatorial explosion,
but it's not in the technical, exponential, sense).  Dennett's
entire schtick here is simply to point out that the circuitry of
our brains appears quite inadequate to the task of providing a
simulation of a full-featured reality -- and yet sometimes it seems
to, in hallucinations and dreams.

His subsequent thoughts about the internal editing and organizing
of sense data always seemed to me similar to Minsky's Society of
Mind, and are quite interesting.

Dennett is not (IMO) qualified or capable of discussing computer
technology or the requirements for simulating physics, except at
the crude level of saying it's Hard.

