Date: Tue, 14 Sep 93 14:27:25 BST
From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON: What's wrong with a service economy?

Tony Hamilton writes:
>But as I said before; why the focus on _jobs_? What about actual output,
>whether service or manufacturing? You can't pay workers unless you have
>money, and you can't get money unless you export more than you import - 
>assuming a society that is not completely self-sufficient.

The world as a whole exports and imports nothing, yet that doesn't seem to
stop anyone getting paid.  The relevance of self-sufficiency is unclear to
me.

I am alas ignorant of macroeconomics, and am moved to ask some very naive
questions, which may be considered too basic to warrant a reply on the
list.

1.  Does GNP mean anything?  Does it matter whether it goes up or down? 
For example, if I am a manufacturer, and I improve my production processes
so that I can sell more goods for a lower price, I may make much more
profit, yet the total value (measured in money) of my production may fall. 
Has GNP gone up or down?  My improved processes may be using less raw
materials.  This may be tough on my suppliers, who may as a result produce
less, decreasing GNP, but as long as they can still supply me, why should I
care?

2.  On a personal level, one wants to get as much money as possible (or
other goods, but money can always buy those other goods).  On the national
level and above, money circulates.  Does it matter which way "the arrow
with the dollar sign on it" points?  Does the general flow of money in or
out of a region matter to any individual living there?

3.  Economic indicators like GNP, trade balance, etc. may be relevant to
the plans one makes as an individual; are they relevant to the plans that a
government makes for the whole country?  Of course, there's no government
like no government, but as we all currently do live under governments, is
there anything they need to do about these things other than disbanding
themselves?

