davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu says:
> > From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
> 
> > but saying "I don't like the fact that my senses
> > might have nothing to do with an objective reality, so I'm going to
> > yell a lot and pretend there is no problem", which is Rand's attitude.
> 
> I shudder at defending Objectivism, but this has gotten far too
> distorted for my taste.
> 
> It is not a fact that my senses might have nothing to do with reality. 
> I will state unequivocally that my senses have to do with reality. I
> may make mistakes in my conclusions from that sense data, but the data
> is real. The sensations are real. I might conclude from what I see
> that something was very big, only to find that close up, it was not.
> My original view of the scene was not invalid, I merely made a false
> conclusion from my sense data.

How do you know that you aren't a brain in a vat, and that any of that
sense data is real sense data and not just simulations that are being
injected directly into your nervous system? 

For all you really know, you have never seen what the real world looks
like. The laws of physics you've grown up with, the vistas you've
seen, everything could just be the imagination of a demented computer
program.

You say "unequivocally" that your senses "have to do with reality". I
would suggest that you are completely unsafe in so doing.

It *IS* safe to say "I have hypothesized that my senses are yielding a
reasonably accurate model of an objective real world, and I have yet
to have seen falsifying evidence for this hypothesis, and I see lots
of positive evidence for it, so I continue to believe it." However, to
hold ANYTHING "unequivocally" is the real denial of reality. Reality
is that you don't actually know the answer and can't, and might as
well admit it, at least to yourself.

Indeed, it has been argued, perhaps accurately, by a Famous Member of
This List, that since in the future we will likely simulate far more
humans than have ever really lived, the odds are actually pretty good
that you are in fact living in a simulation, even ignoring the
possibility that our entire universe is a simulation.

